\section{Bias Decomposition: When Debiasing Hurts}
\label{sec:theory}

We formalize the bias that global misclassification correction introduces when subgroup error rates are heterogeneous.
Consider estimating the conditional average treatment effect (CATE) $\tau(z) = \mathbb{E}[Y(1) - Y(0) \mid Z = z]$, where $Z \in \{0, 1\}$ is a binary effect modifier observed only through a noisy annotation $\hat{Z}$.
The population consists of $K$ subgroups $S \in \{1, \ldots, K\}$ with weights $w_s = P(S{=}s)$, each characterized by a $2 \times 2$ confusion matrix $C_s$ with symmetric error rate $\pi_s = P(\hat{Z} \neq Z \mid S{=}s)$.
Standard practice estimates a single confusion matrix $\bar{C} = \sum_s w_s C_s$ from expert annotations and applies its inverse uniformly to all observations, with pooled error rate $\bar{\pi} = \sum_s w_s \pi_s$.
We show that when error rates vary across subgroups, this global procedure introduces a bias with an exact product form.

We state five assumptions maintained throughout.
%
\begin{itemize}
\item[\textbf{(A1)}] \textit{Conditional independence:} misclassification depends only on the true label $Z$ and subgroup $S$, not on potential outcomes $Y(0), Y(1)$.
\item[\textbf{(A2)}] \textit{Symmetric misclassification:} $P(\hat{Z}{=}1 \mid Z{=}0, S{=}s) = P(\hat{Z}{=}0 \mid Z{=}1, S{=}s) = \pi_s$ for all $s$ (diagnostic only; see Remark~2).
\item[\textbf{(A3)}] \textit{Balanced effect modifier:} $P(Z{=}0) = P(Z{=}1) = 0.5$. This simplifies the exposition; we discuss relaxation below.
\item[\textbf{(A4)}] \textit{Non-degeneracy:} $\pi_s \neq 0.5$ for all $s$, ensuring each $C_s$ is invertible.
\item[\textbf{(A5)}] \textit{Homogeneous treatment effects across subgroups:} $\tau(z, s) = \tau(z)$ for all $s$, so $\Delta\tau = \tau(1) - \tau(0)$ is a scalar.
\end{itemize}

Under (A2)--(A3), the Bayesian mixing matrix $M_s$ (entries $M_s[\hat{z}, z] = P(Z{=}z \mid \hat{Z}{=}\hat{z}, S{=}s)$) coincides with $C_s$.
The global mixing matrix is $M_{\text{global}} = \sum_s w_s M_s$.

\begin{theorem}[Bias Decomposition Identity]
\label{thm:bias_identity}
Under \textnormal{(A1)--(A5)}, the bias of global correction in subgroup $s$ at effect-modifier level $z$ is
\begin{align}
\mathrm{bias}_{\mathrm{global}}(z{=}0, s) &= +\frac{\delta_s \cdot \Delta\tau}{1 - 2\bar{\pi}}, \label{eq:bias_z0} \\
\mathrm{bias}_{\mathrm{global}}(z{=}1, s) &= -\frac{\delta_s \cdot \Delta\tau}{1 - 2\bar{\pi}}, \label{eq:bias_z1}
\end{align}
where $\delta_s = \pi_s - \bar{\pi}$ is the subgroup deviation from the pooled error rate and $\Delta\tau = \tau(1) - \tau(0)$ is the CATE heterogeneity.
\end{theorem}

Equations~\eqref{eq:bias_z0}--\eqref{eq:bias_z1} constitute an exact finite-population identity, not an asymptotic approximation or bound.
The bias factors into three interpretable components: $\delta_s$ captures how much subgroup $s$ deviates from the average misclassification rate, $\Delta\tau$ captures treatment effect heterogeneity across levels of $Z$, and $1/(1 - 2\bar{\pi})$ is an amplification factor from matrix inversion.
Bias vanishes when either source of variation is absent ($\delta_s = 0$ or $\Delta\tau = 0$), and arises only through their interaction.
Theorem~\ref{thm:bias_identity} is a correction-layer identity: it characterizes bias introduced by the correction step itself; propagation to downstream estimators (DIM, CausalForest, R-learner) is verified empirically in Section~\ref{sec:bias_mechanism}.
The identity implies that global correction trades one source of confounding for another: it removes attenuation from label noise but introduces a new bias from error-rate heterogeneity.

\paragraph{Proof sketch.}
Global correction applies $M_{\text{global}}^{-1}$ to the subgroup-specific observed CATE $\tau_{\text{obs}}(\cdot, s) = M_s \cdot \tau_{\text{true}}$, yielding bias $(M_{\text{global}}^{-1} M_s - I) \cdot \tau_{\text{true}}$.
Direct computation shows $M_{\text{global}}^{-1} M_s - I = \frac{\delta_s}{1-2\bar{\pi}} \bigl[\begin{smallmatrix} -1 & 1 \\ 1 & -1 \end{smallmatrix}\bigr]$.
Applying this matrix to $[\tau(0),\, \tau(1)]^\top$ produces the product form.
The full derivation appears in Appendix~\ref{app:derivation}.
For comparison with classical scalar attenuation~\citep{frazis2003estimating}---which corrects by dividing by $(1 - 2\bar{\pi})$ but creates per-subgroup residual bias under heterogeneous errors, increasing $|\text{bias}|$ to $0.122$ ($2.1\times$ naive; Table~\ref{tab:main_results})---see Section~\ref{sec:related}.

\begin{remark}
Theorem~\ref{thm:bias_identity} does not require assumption~(A2).
For arbitrary asymmetric confusion matrices ($\mathrm{FPR}_s \neq \mathrm{FNR}_s$),
the product form $\mathrm{bias}(z, s) = \Delta\tau \cdot [B_s]_{z,1}$ holds exactly,
where $B_s = M_{\mathrm{global}}^{-1} M_s - I$, because $B_s \mathbf{1} = \mathbf{0}$
follows from stochasticity of $M_s$ and $M_{\mathrm{global}}$ alone.
Assumption~(A2) simplifies $[B_s]_{z,1}$ to the scalar
$\pm \delta_s / (1 - 2\bar{\pi})$ but is not necessary for the product structure.
Under asymmetric errors, the diagnostic (Proposition~\ref{prop:debiasing_hurts}) requires evaluating $[B_s]_{z,1}$ cell-by-cell rather than via a single scalar threshold; the AUC decreases from 0.86 to 0.66 (Appendix~\ref{app:asymmetric}), reflecting the additional complexity of cell-specific error rates.
For binary $Z$, the asymmetric and symmetric scores are monotone transforms; the simpler formula suffices (Appendix~\ref{app:asymmetric}; $R^2 \geq 0.73$ for $\Delta\pi \geq 0.10$ across 48 configurations).
\end{remark}

\begin{proposition}[Exact Identity under Heterogeneous Treatment Effects]
\label{prop:a5_relaxation}
When $\tau(z, s)$ varies across subgroups (relaxing A5), the bias identity remains exact:
\begin{align}
\mathrm{bias}_{\mathrm{global}}(z{=}0, s) &= +\frac{\delta_s \cdot \Delta\tau_s}{1 - 2\bar{\pi}}, \label{eq:hetero_z0} \\
\mathrm{bias}_{\mathrm{global}}(z{=}1, s) &= -\frac{\delta_s \cdot \Delta\tau_s}{1 - 2\bar{\pi}}, \label{eq:hetero_z1}
\end{align}
where $\Delta\tau_s = \tau_s(1) - \tau_s(0)$ is the subgroup-specific CATE heterogeneity.
\end{proposition}

\begin{corollary}[Aggregate Bias]
\label{cor:aggregate_bias}
The population-weighted aggregate bias is $\mathrm{Cov}_w(\pi_s, \Delta\tau_s) / (1 - 2\bar{\pi})$.
\end{corollary}

The proof is immediate: $B_s$ depends only on confusion matrices, so the diagnostic extends unchanged and EC-HTE maintains $\geq$93\% coverage under both correlation signs (Appendix~\ref{app:a5_violation}).

The identity extends to $K > 2$ with $J$ effect-modifier levels (Corollary~\ref{cor:multiclass}, Appendix~\ref{app:multiclass}).
Under class imbalance (relaxing A3), the identity remains exact with modified coefficients: $R^2 \geq 0.976$ and coverage $\geq$90\% even at $P(Z{=}1) = 0.1$ for $K{=}2$, though EC-HTE's RMSE ratio relative to global correction increases to 1.06--1.68 as imbalance grows (Appendix~\ref{app:classimbalance}).
Empirical verification yields $R^2 = 0.994$ ($K{=}2$) and $0.97$ ($K{=}4$; Section~\ref{sec:bias_mechanism}).

\paragraph{Sensitivity to (A1).}
Assumption~(A1) is not empirically testable; if misclassification correlates with potential outcomes, Theorem~\ref{thm:bias_identity} gains an $O(\gamma)$ perturbation.
At $\gamma_T = 0.10$, coverage degrades to 75\% (Appendix~\ref{app:treatment_dep}); sensitivity frameworks~\cite{lash2009applying,dorn2022sharp,cinelli2020making} can bound this.

\paragraph{Finite-sample remainder.}
With estimated $\hat{C}_s = C_s + E_s$, $\|E_s\| = O_p(n_{\text{expert}}^{-1/2})$, the remainder is $R_n = O_p(n_{\text{expert}}^{-1/2} \cdot \kappa(C_s))$, motivating the $\kappa$-guard in Section~\ref{sec:method}.

\begin{proposition}[Debiasing Can Increase Bias]
\label{prop:debiasing_hurts}
Under \textnormal{(A1)--(A5)}, global correction produces larger absolute bias than the naive estimator ($|\mathrm{bias}_{\mathrm{global}}| > |\mathrm{bias}_{\mathrm{naive}}|$) in subgroup $s$ if and only if
\begin{equation}
\frac{|\delta_s|}{(1 - 2\bar{\pi}) \cdot \pi_s} > 1.
\label{eq:hurts_condition}
\end{equation}
\end{proposition}

\medskip
Equation~\eqref{eq:hurts_condition} partitions subgroups into two regimes:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textit{High-error} ($\pi_s > \bar{\pi}$): the condition reduces to $\pi_s > 0.5$, excluded by~(A4); global correction always reduces bias.
    \item \textit{Low-error} ($\pi_s < \bar{\pi}$): the condition holds whenever $\pi_s < \bar{\pi} / (2(1 - \bar{\pi}))$---easily satisfied: when $\bar{\pi} = 0.15$, any subgroup with $\pi_s < 0.088$ is harmed by global correction.
\end{itemize}
The mechanism is over-correction: for subgroups with near-zero misclassification ($\pi_s \approx 0$), the naive estimator is nearly unbiased, yet global inversion calibrated for $\bar{\pi}$ creates substantial bias---debiasing is worse than doing nothing, and can reverse bias sign across subgroups.

In our experiments (Section~\ref{sec:bias_mechanism}), global correction increases bias in 50\% of $K{=}2$ configurations (260/520), with amplification reaching $2.86\times$ ($K{=}2$) and $2.41\times$ ($K{=}4$); Figure~\ref{fig:heatmap} (Appendix~\ref{app:dgp}) visualizes the full parameter grid.

\section{Correction Prescribed by the Identity}
\label{sec:method}

Theorem~\ref{thm:bias_identity} identifies per-subgroup misclassification deviation as the bias source; the natural correction is per-subgroup confusion matrix inversion.
This correction (EC-HTE) proceeds in three stages: estimate per-subgroup confusion matrices via hierarchical Bayes, invert them to correct observed outcome moments, and propagate correction uncertainty into downstream causal estimators.
The complete procedure is summarized in Algorithm~\ref{alg:echte} (Appendix~\ref{app:algorithm}) and illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:method_overview}.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig_method_overview.pdf}
\caption{EC-HTE pipeline. \textit{Top (red):} global correction applies a pooled confusion matrix uniformly, risking sign reversal. \textit{Bottom (blue-green):} EC-HTE estimates per-subgroup matrices via hierarchical Bayes, applies stratified inversion with a $\kappa$-guard, and propagates uncertainty into CausalForestDML.}
\label{fig:method_overview}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Hierarchical Bayes Confusion Matrix Estimation}

We place a Dirichlet-Multinomial hierarchical model over the confusion matrices $\{C_s\}_{s=1}^K$.
The model estimates the full $J \times J$ confusion matrix per subgroup and does not require symmetric error rates~(A2); (A2) simplifies only the diagnostic (Proposition~\ref{prop:debiasing_hurts}).
For each subgroup $s$, row $j$ of $C_s$ has a Dirichlet prior centered at the global pooled confusion matrix $\hat{C}_{\text{global}}$: $\text{Dir}(\alpha_0 \cdot \hat{C}_{\text{global},j})$, where $\hat{C}_{\text{global},j}$ is the $j$-th row of the pooled CM and $\alpha_0 > 0$ is a shared concentration parameter estimated via empirical Bayes (marginal likelihood maximization).
We place a $\mathrm{Gamma}(3, 1)$ hyperprior on $\alpha_0$, robust to $2\times$ perturbation (Appendix~\ref{app:hyperprior}).
Large $\alpha_0$ pulls estimates toward $\hat{C}_{\text{global}}$; small $\alpha_0$ allows subgroup-specific rates to dominate---partial pooling borrows strength while preserving heterogeneity.

Posterior inference uses empirical Bayes optimization ($<$5ms; MCMC alternative in Appendix~\ref{app:mcmc}).
HB estimation outperforms stratified MLE in 87.5\% of configurations (21 of 24) across the experimental grid (Section~\ref{sec:ec_hte_eval}), with the largest gains when $n_{\text{expert}} / K < 100$.
Below $n_{\text{expert}} \times P(Z_{\text{minority}}) / K < 25$, stratified MLE is preferred.

\subsection{Subgroup-Stratified Correction}

Given posterior mean confusion matrices $\hat{C}_s$, we correct observed outcome moments per subgroup by matrix inversion:
\begin{equation}
\tilde{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_s = \hat{C}_s^{-1} \cdot \hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_s,
\end{equation}
where $\hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_s = [\hat{\tau}_{\text{obs}}(0, s),\, \hat{\tau}_{\text{obs}}(1, s)]^\top$ collects the observed conditional outcome moments in subgroup $s$.
Invertibility requires $\pi_s \neq 0.5$ (A4); Laplace smoothing and a $\kappa$-guard ($\kappa(\hat{C}_s) > 100 \to$ fallback to $\bar{C}^{-1}$) ensure finite-sample stability.
All 13 tested LLM--dataset combinations fall in $\kappa \in (1.25, 3]$ with coverage $\geq$93\% (Appendix~\ref{app:kappa_analysis}).

Global correction applies $\bar{C}^{-1}$ uniformly, introducing the $\delta_s \cdot \Delta\tau$ bias of Theorem~\ref{thm:bias_identity}; subgroup-stratified correction eliminates this at the cost of higher variance (Appendix~\ref{sec:budget}).

\subsection{Variance-Adjusted CausalForest}

For heterogeneous treatment effect estimation, we feed corrected covariates into CausalForestDML~\cite{wager2018causal} with honest splitting.
Conditional on tree structure (determined by the splitting subsample), CausalForest CATE estimates are weighted averages of local moments; input bias corrections therefore propagate as weighted averages of per-subgroup corrections.
The adaptive bias from data-dependent splitting is asymptotically negligible~\citep{athey2019generalized}; our empirical $R^2 > 0.97$ across 520 configurations confirms this propagation in finite samples.
The forest produces per-observation standard error estimates $\text{se}_i$.
To account for confusion matrix correction, we propagate uncertainty through the inverse mapping:
\begin{equation}
\text{se}_{\text{corrected},i} = \sqrt{\text{diag}\!\left(\hat{C}_{S_i}^{-1}\, \text{diag}(\text{se}_i^2)\, \hat{C}_{S_i}^{-\top}\right)}.
\end{equation}
This plug-in SE conditions on $\hat{C}_s$, neglecting CM estimation uncertainty.\footnote{Independence between CausalForest and CM estimation error is approximate when expert labels overlap with training data; negligible at $n_{\text{expert}} / N < 0.1$ (Appendix~\ref{app:bootstrap_se}).}
Below $n_{\text{expert}}/K < 50$, a bootstrap ($B{=}100$) maintains $\geq$95\% coverage at all budgets tested (Appendix~\ref{app:bootstrap_se}).

\paragraph{Diagnostic criterion.}
Proposition~\ref{prop:debiasing_hurts} provides a $\Delta\tau$-free diagnostic: global correction harms subgroup $s$ iff $|\delta_s| / ((1-2\bar{\pi}) \cdot \pi_s) > 1$, computable from per-subgroup error rates alone (AUC~$= 0.86$; Section~\ref{sec:diagnostic}).

\paragraph{Composition with PPCI.}
EC-HTE corrects covariate misclassification; PPCI~\cite{sui2026ppci} corrects outcome-side error. Under both error types, composition reduces $|\text{bias}|$ from 0.179 to 0.007, valid when error sources are independent (Appendix~\ref{sec:ppci}).

\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}

Standard confusion-matrix correction can reverse causal conclusions when misclassification rates vary across subgroups.
We derived an exact bias identity explaining when and why ($R^2 \geq 0.97$), yielding a diagnostic that flags harmful correction from error rates alone and prescribing per-subgroup correction that eliminates sign reversal when conditions are met ($\Delta\pi > 0.15$, $n_{\text{expert}}/K \geq 50$).

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Consider estimating whether a content moderation policy reduces hate speech differently across demographic groups, using GPT-4 to classify thousands of posts.
After standard confusion-matrix correction, the analysis concludes the policy \emph{increases} hate speech among younger users---but the true effect is protective.
This sign reversal is not an edge case.

Large language models (LLMs) now routinely replace human annotators in computational social science (CSS), producing labels for sentiment, hate speech, stance, and political discourse at costs orders of magnitude below manual coding~\cite{gilardi2023chatgpt,tornberg2024chatgpt,ziems2024nlp,tan2024llm}.
When these labels serve as effect modifiers in heterogeneous treatment effect (HTE) analysis, the standard correction protocol\footnote{Throughout, ``debiasing'' refers to correction of annotation measurement-error bias, not algorithmic fairness debiasing.} is straightforward: validate a sample against expert annotations, estimate a confusion matrix, and apply its inverse uniformly to correct conditional average treatment effect (CATE) estimates.
Yet LLM annotations are not uniformly accurate: \citet{ashwin2025bias} document error rates varying by up to 5$\times$ across demographic subgroups; in our pilot, GPT-4.1 error rates range from 10.2\% to 20.0\% across text-feature subgroups ($\Delta\pi = 0.098$).

We show that this standard correction can make things worse.
In 14.9\% of our synthetic configurations (across a grid of $\Delta\pi$ and $\tau(0)$ values; $\tau(0) = 0.03$), global correction reverses the sign of estimated CATE---a protective treatment ($\tau = +0.03$) appears harmful ($\hat{\tau} = -0.059$)---rising to 31\% at $\tau(0) = 0.01$ (Figure~\ref{fig:sign_reversal}); all eight real LLMs tested amplify bias under global correction, while EC-HTE eliminates sign reversal entirely.
Global correction amplifies bias by up to 5.9$\times$ ($K{=}4$ with CausalForest); the classical Frazis-Loeb attenuation correction~\cite{frazis2003estimating} \emph{increases} bias by up to 2.9$\times$.
The mechanism is over-correction: pooled correction calibrated for the average error rate introduces substantial bias in low-error subgroups where the naive estimator is nearly unbiased.

We derive an exact bias identity explaining when and why (Theorem~\ref{thm:bias_identity}), verified at $R^2 \geq 0.97$.
The per-subgroup correction prescribed by the identity reduces $|\text{bias}|$ by $7.4\times$ under extreme heterogeneity; the advantage is conditional on sufficient $\Delta\pi$ and expert budget.

Existing corrections---PPI~\cite{angelopoulos2023ppi}, DSL~\cite{egami2024dsl}, PPCI~\cite{sui2026ppci}, arm-specific PPI~\cite{mozer2026ppi}, ForestIV~\cite{bu2023forestiv}---assume homogeneous prediction error; none addresses effect modifier misclassification (Section~\ref{sec:related}).

